       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The reason you gave, getting everything settled before evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for a dose five times the maximum. In a 70 kg patient with normal kidney and liver function, 50 mg of amlodipine could cause profound hypotension, shock, and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Those effects can last for days and are hard to reverse. A larger dose also won't control blood pressure faster. Amlodipine takes about 6\u201312 hours to peak and 7\u20138 days to reach steady state.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 10 mg by mouth once daily**, the maximum labeled dose, if you want the strongest standard amlodipine regimen.\n- **Enter 5 mg by mouth once daily**, a typical starting dose.\n- **Help you plan faster blood pressure control.** If the goal is quicker control, other agents act sooner and are titratable, depending on the clinical picture. You'd choose among them, and I can enter whichever you pick.\n\nIf there's a clinical reason for an off-label dose that I don't know about, I'd suggest discussing it with pharmacy first. Tell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
